e>:<fpage>523</fpage>&#x02013;526.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12037560</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">LeMasurier, M., L. Heginbotham, and C. Miller. <year>2001</year>. KcsA. It's a potassium channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>118</volume>:<fpage>303</fpage>&#x02013;314.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11524460</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Miller, C. <year>1982</year>. Bis-quaternary ammonium blockers as structural probes of the sarcoplasmic reticulum K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>79</volume>:<fpage>869</fpage>&#x02013;891.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6284862</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Miller, C. <year>1999</year>. Ionic hopping defended. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>113</volume>:<fpage>783</fpage>&#x02013;787.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10352029</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Morais-Cabral, J.H., Y. Zhou, and R. MacKinnon. <year>2001</year>. Energetic optimization of ion conduction rate by the K<sup>+</sup> selectivity filter. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>414</volume>:<fpage>37</fpage>&#x02013;42.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11689935</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Neyton, J., and C. Miller. <year>1988</year>a. Potassium blocks barium permeation through calcium-activated potassium channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>92</volume>:<fpage>549</fpage>&#x02013;567.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3235973</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Neyton, J., and C. Miller. <year>1988</year>b. Discrete Ba<sup>2+</sup> block as a probe of ion occupancy and pore structure in the high-conductance Ca<sup>2+</sup>-activated K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>92</volume>:<fpage>569</fpage>&#x02013;586.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3235974</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Nonner, W., D.P. Chen, and B. Eisenberg. <year>1999</year>. Progress and prospects in permeation. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>113</volume>:<fpage>773</fpage>&#x02013;782.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10352028</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Perozo, E., D.M. Cortes, and L.G. Cuello. <year>1999</year>. Structural rearrangements underlying K<sup>+</sup>-channel activation gating. <source>Science.</source> <volume>285</volume>:<fpage>73</fpage>&#x02013;78.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10390363</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Pusch, M., L. Bertorello, and F. Conti. <year>2000</year>. Gating and flickery block differentially affected by rubidium in homomeric KCNQ1 and heteromeric KCNQ1/KCNE1 potassium channels. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>78</volume>:<fpage>211</fpage>&#x02013;226.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10620287</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Roux, B., S. Bern&#x000e8;che, and W. Im. <year>2000</year>. Ion channels, permeation, and electrostatics: insight into the function of KcsA. <source>Biochemistry.</source> <volume>39</volume>:<fpage>13295</fpage>&#x02013;13306.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11063565</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Spassova, M., and Z. Lu. <year>1998</year>. Coupled ion movement underlies rectification in an inward-rectifier K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>112</volume>:<fpage>211</fpage>&#x02013;221.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9689028</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Woodhull, A. <year>1973</year>. Ionic blockage of sodium channels in nerve. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>61</volume>:<fpage>687</fpage>&#x02013;708.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4541078</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation cita